      "page_idx": 26,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.088,
            0.489,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Remainder is 1 so p = 30k + 1 Possible values are 31, 61, 91 91 is divisible by 7. The number of pebbles is 91."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.178,
            0.848,
            0.235
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Tathagat has written several numbers that leave a remainder of 2 when divided by 6. He claims, “If you add any three such numbers, the sum will always be a multiple of 6.” Is Tathagat’s claim true?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.241,
            0.62,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let three numbers be 6a + 2, 6b + 2, 6c + 2. Add them and check for yourself. Yes Tathagat’s claim is true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.31,
            0.848,
            0.387
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. When divided by 7, the number 661 leaves a remainder of 3, and 4779 leaves a remainder of 5. Without calculating, can you say what remainders the following expressions will leave when divided by 7? Show the solution both algebraically and visually."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.203,
            0.385,
            0.506,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) 4779 + 661 (ii) 4779 – 661"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.411,
            0.339,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. Algebraically"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
            0.435,
            0.388,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) 4779 = 7p + 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.476,
            0.522,
            0.515
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_26_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}4779 + 661 &= 7p + 7q + 8 \\\\&= 7(p + q + 1) + 1\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.515,
            0.436,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So remainder is 1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.538,
            0.521,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\begin{aligned}4779 - 661 &= 7p + 5 - 7q - 3 \\\\&= 7(p - q) + 2\\end{aligned}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " So remainder is 2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.599,
            0.656,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For visual method refer P. 120 of the textbook."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.628,
            0.848,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Find a number that leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 3, a remainder of 3 when divided by 4, and a remainder of 4 when divided by 5. What is the smallest such number? Can you give a simple explanation of why it is the smallest?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.71,
            0.848,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Given each remainder is 1 less than its divisor. So the number must be one less from LCM of 3, 4, 5. LCM of 3, 4, 5 = 3 × 4 × 5 = 60 So smallest number is 60 − 1 = 59, since 60 is the smallest value of multiples of 3, 4 and 5."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.917,
            0.881,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
